# Foundations, metrics and position sensing.
ask(1622,'sensor-role','Environment and internal-state bullets','A robot executes a fixed motor schedule without measurements. What can it not establish from that schedule alone?', [('Whether an unobserved obstacle has entered its path','No observation updates the prescribed motion when the environment changes.'),('Whether its stored command sequence has a next instruction','A stored schedule can contain its own next instruction without sensing.'),('Whether it can issue any motor command at all','Open-loop commands are possible; sensing is needed for the missing environmental evidence.')],kind='scenario',conflict=ROLE)
ask(1623,'sensor-role','Six functional roles of sensors','Which chain describes closed-loop position control?', [('Measure position → compare with target → adjust actuation','Measured error informs the control action.'),('Choose a target → replay the same command regardless of measurement','This is open-loop replay, not feedback adjustment.'),('Measure position → store the reading without changing the command','Logging alone does not close the actuation loop.')],kind='sequence')
ask(1623,'sensor-role','Safety, learning, localization and manipulation bullets','Which pairing correctly links a sensing role to its immediate use?', [('Localization: estimate pose from observations; manipulation: measure object/contact state','These roles support pose estimation and physical interaction respectively.'),('Localization: directly generate motor torque; manipulation: replace contact feedback with a map','Pose observations are not themselves torque commands, and a map does not replace contact feedback.'),('Localization: measure only motor current; manipulation: determine only satellite position','These measurements do not uniquely define the stated roles.')],kind='match')
ask(1624,'taxonomy','Proprioceptive and exteroceptive lists','Which pair best illustrates the slide’s internal-state versus external-environment distinction?', [('Joint encoder angle; camera image of a nearby object','Joint position is internal configuration; the scene image concerns the environment.'),('Camera image of a nearby object; joint encoder angle','The ordering reverses external and internal observations.'),('LiDAR obstacle range; GPS position as a joint angle','LiDAR is external and GPS does not measure an individual joint angle.')],kind='match')
ask(1624,'taxonomy','Joint torque versus force/tactile list entries','Why can a force-related signal require context before assigning it a single taxonomy label?', [('Joint torque may describe internal loading, while contact force describes interaction with the environment','Classification follows the measured role and placement, not merely the word force.'),('Every force signal must be internal because a wire enters the robot','Wiring location does not determine the physical quantity being sensed.'),('Every torque signal must be external because torque has units','Units do not resolve internal state versus external interaction.')])
ask(1625,'performance-static','Range, span and signed-error definitions','A sensor range is −20 to 80 °C. It reports 31 °C when the reference is 30 °C. What are span and signed error?', [('100 °C; +1 °C','Span is80−(−20)=100°C; measured minus reference is31−30=+1°C.'),('60 °C; +1 °C','This adds signed endpoints instead of subtracting the minimum.'),('100 °C; −1 °C','This reverses the specified error sign.')],kind='numerical',steps=['Subtract lower endpoint from upper endpoint.','Subtract reference value from reported value.'],formulas=[fm('S=x_max−x_min; e=x_measured−x_reference',{'S':'Span','x_max':'Upper range limit','x_min':'Lower range limit','e':'Signed error','x_measured':'Reported temperature','x_reference':'Reference temperature'},{'S,e,x_max,x_min,x_measured,x_reference':'°C'},['Same temperature scale; error defined as measured minus reference.'])])
ask(1625,'performance-static','Accuracy versus precision definitions','Repeated readings are 12.00, 12.01 and 11.99 N when a trusted reference is 10.00 N. Which description fits?', [('High repeatability with a positive bias relative to the reference','The readings cluster tightly but are about2N too high.'),('High accuracy but low precision','Tight clustering is precision; disagreement with reference is an accuracy problem.'),('Low repeatability with approximately zero bias','The spread is small and the offset is clearly positive.')],kind='scenario',ass=['The reference uncertainty is negligible relative to the2N offset.'])
ask(1625,'performance-static','Resolution definition','Which specification describes resolution rather than accuracy?', [('Smallest input change distinguishable in the output','Resolution concerns distinguishability, not closeness to truth.'),('Maximum deviation from a calibrated reference','That is an accuracy/error specification.'),('Spread among repeated measurements of one fixed input','That concerns repeatability or precision.')],kind='definition')
ask(1625,'performance-static','Dead band definition','An output stays at 2 V while the input varies from 4 to 5 mm, then changes beyond that interval. What illustrates the dead band?', [('The input interval produces no responsive output change, despite a nonzero baseline','Dead band need not mean the electrical output is literally zero.'),('The output must be zero throughout the interval','A2V plateau is compatible with no response to small input changes.'),('The interval is necessarily a communication delay measured in seconds','The supplied interval is in input displacement, not time.')],conflict=DEAD)
ask(1625,'performance-static','Dead time and bandwidth definitions','Which comparison is correct?', [('Dead time is a delay before response begins; bandwidth describes tracked signal frequencies','The first is temporal onset, the second is frequency response.'),('Dead time is the smallest input step; bandwidth is measurement error','Those are resolution and error, not the named properties.'),('Bandwidth equals ADC sampling rate for every sensor','Sampling and physical/electronic response bandwidth are distinct constraints.')])
ask(1626,'performance-dynamic','Sensitivity definition','A linear sensor output rises from 1 to 4 V as force rises from 10 to 40 N. What is its sensitivity?', [('0.10 V/N','Output change3V divided by input change30N gives0.10V/N.'),('10 N/V','This is inverse sensitivity, with reversed units.'),('0.075 V/N','This divides output change by the final force rather than force change.')],kind='numerical',steps=['Δy=4−1=3V.','Δx=40−10=30N.','Sensitivity=3/30=0.10V/N.'],formulas=[fm('K=Δy/Δx',{'K':'Sensitivity','Δy':'Output-voltage change','Δx':'Force change'},{'K':'V/N','Δy':'V','Δx':'N'},['Linear response over the specified interval.'])])
ask(1626,'performance-dynamic','Hysteresis and noise definitions','At the same input, an increasing sweep gives 2.2 V and a decreasing sweep gives 2.0 V reproducibly. Which effect is indicated?', [('Hysteresis','The output depends on the input history or sweep direction.'),('Independent random noise only','A reproducible directional split is not explained by random noise alone.'),('A constant calibration offset only','One constant offset does not explain two outputs for the same input.')])
ask(1626,'performance-dynamic','Latency, linearity and noise definitions','Which statements are valid? Select all that apply.', [('Latency can include acquisition and communication time before a result is available','End-to-end availability can occur after sensing begins.'),('An affine calibration can include an offset as well as a constant slope','A nonzero intercept does not by itself prevent a straight-line calibration.'),('Averaging repeated values necessarily removes a fixed calibration bias','A fixed bias survives averaging unless separately corrected.'),('Random variation and systematic bias are different error effects','Noise scatter and offset require different interpretation.')],correct=(0,1,3))
ask(1627,'potentiometer','Three-terminal working principle','Which terminal provides the position-dependent divider output?', [('The wiper between the supply-connected track endpoints','Wiper position selects a fraction of the track voltage.'),('Either fixed endpoint, regardless of shaft position','An ideally supplied endpoint remains at its rail voltage.'),('The ground terminal only after converting it to a pulse output','The stated sensor is an analog divider, not a pulse encoder.')])
ask(1627,'potentiometer','Voltage proportional to position and applications','For a linear potentiometer to provide a useful voltage-to-angle mapping, which assumptions matter?', [('Known angular calibration and sufficiently small electrical loading','Track linearity and loading determine the divider relationship.'),('Only the serial baud rate; track shape and loading do not matter','Baud rate does not establish the analog calibration.'),('A digital threshold output is required before any angle can be estimated','An ADC can sample the analog wiper voltage directly.')],kind='scenario')
ask(1628,'potentiometer-code','Pin table and analogRead/println lines','Which acquisition path is printed?', [('Wiper to A0; analogRead returns a count; Serial.println sends it','The table and loop show that sequence.'),('Wiper to digital2; digitalRead returns an angle in degrees','Neither the pin nor the return quantity matches the code.'),('Wiper to A0; analogWrite generates the measured position','analogWrite is an output function and does not perform this reading.')],kind='algorithm_trace',ck='code',steps=['Read the wiper onA0.','Store its ADC count in potValue.','Print that count followed by a newline.'])
ask(1628,'potentiometer-code','0–1023 code range and ADC explanation','Under an ideal 10-bit, 0–5 V uniform-bin model, how many codes and what bin width are available?', [('1024 codes; 4.8828125 mV','2^10=1024 and5/1024V=4.8828125mV.'),('1023 codes; 4.8875855 mV','1023 is the largest code, not the count of codes; this uses endpoint interpolation.'),('1024 codes; 9.765625 mV','This doubles the correct bin width.')],kind='numerical',ass=['Uniform-bin quantizer;5V reference; no noise or differential nonlinearity.'],steps=['Codes run0through1023 inclusive.','Compute5V/1024 and convertV to mV.'],formulas=[fm('N=2^b; ΔV=V_ref/N',{'N':'Number of ADC codes','b':'Resolution bits','ΔV':'Ideal bin width','V_ref':'Reference voltage'},{'N':'dimensionless','b':'bits','ΔV,V_ref':'V'},['Uniform2^b-bin model, not endpoint interpolation.'])],conflict=ADCQ)
ask(1628,'potentiometer-code','delay(200) and Serial.begin(9600)','Ignoring all other work, delay(200) permits at most approximately what loop rate?', [('5 updates/s','A200ms wait is0.2s, so1/0.2=5/s.'),('200 updates/s','The delay argument is milliseconds per wait, not updates per second.'),('9600 updates/s','9600 is serial baud, not loop frequency.')],kind='numerical',ck='code',ass=['Neglect ADC, serial and loop overhead; actual loop rate is lower.'],steps=['Convert200ms to0.2s.','Take the reciprocal for the delay-limited rate.'],formulas=[fm('f_max≈1/T_delay',{'f_max':'Delay-only upper approximation to update rate','T_delay':'Per-loop delay'},{'f_max':'Hz','T_delay':'s'},['Other work neglected; not guaranteed actual sampling rate.'])])
ask(1629,'potentiometer-demo','Repeated541 Serial Monitor readout','Use an explicitly supplied endpoint calibration: code0 means0°, code1023 means270°. What angle does the pictured code541 estimate?', [('142.79°','270×541/1023≈142.79° under this supplied endpoint calibration.'),('243.45°','This treats600counts as a full270° scale instead of1023.'),('541°','The raw ADC count is not already an angle.')],kind='numerical',ck='figure',ass=['Linear endpoint interpolation, no loading or calibration error; this270° scale is supplied, not visible in the photo.'],steps=['Use fraction541/1023 of the calibrated angular travel.','Multiply by270° and round to two decimals.'],formulas=[fm('θ=θ_min+(n/n_max)(θ_max−θ_min)',{'θ':'Estimated angle','θ_min,θ_max':'Calibrated angular endpoints','n':'ADC code','n_max':'Upper endpoint code'},{'θ,θ_min,θ_max':'degrees','n,n_max':'dimensionless'},['Linear endpoint calibration;0≤n≤n_max.'])])
ask(1630,'encoder','Incremental and absolute encoder comparison','After power-up at an unknown position, which sensor supplies a single-turn encoded sector without first counting from home?', [('A suitable absolute encoder','Its sector code identifies position within the encoded turn.'),('An incremental encoder using only a reset pulse count','A reset count lacks an absolute origin until a reference is established.'),('Either type automatically supplies all previous full turns after power loss','Single-turn encoding does not automatically retain multi-turn history.')])
ask(1630,'encoder','Four-track sixteen-sector absolute disk','Assuming the drawn four-track disk has uniformly spaced distinct binary sectors, what are the number of sectors and angular interval?', [('16 sectors; 22.5°','Four bits encode16states and360/16=22.5°.'),('4 sectors; 90°','This mistakes tracks for the number of code combinations.'),('16 sectors; 11.25°','This uses32intervals instead of16.')],kind='numerical',ck='figure',ass=['All16codes are used once per turn with uniform sector widths.'],steps=['Compute2^4=16codes.','Divide360°by16.'],formulas=[fm('N=2^b; Δθ=360°/N',{'N':'Distinct sectors','b':'Binary tracks/bits','Δθ':'Uniform angular interval'},{'N':'dimensionless','b':'bits','Δθ':'degrees'},['All code combinations used uniformly in one revolution.'])])
ask(1630,'encoder','Optical transmitter, slotted disk and receiver drawing','What produces incremental pulses in the pictured optical arrangement?', [('Alternating interruption and passage of light through the rotating disk','The receiver sees changing transmission as slots pass.'),('The shaft directly changes the receiver into a potentiometer','The diagram shows optical interruption, not a resistive track.'),('Every opaque segment produces an absolute multi-turn address','A pulse train alone does not supply that absolute history.')],kind='diagram',ck='figure')
ask(1631,'quadrature','A/B channels and pin table','What does the relative phase of channels A and B add to a pulse count?', [('A direction indication under a defined wiring/sign convention','Which channel leads distinguishes opposite rotation directions.'),('An absolute multi-turn origin without any reference','Relative phase is not a persistent absolute origin.'),('A supply-voltage calibration independent of rotation','Phase describes timing, not analog supply calibration.')])
